{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_5","type":"agent_message","text":"{\"answers\":[{\"question_id\":\"Q016_pdb_1acc_domain_split\",\"answer\":\"In SCOP 1.75, PDB 1acc chain A was a single SCOP domain, d1acca_, classified as f.11.1.1: class f, Membrane and cell surface proteins and peptides; fold f.11, Anthrax protective antigen; superfamily f.11.1, Anthrax protective antigen; family f.11.1.1, Anthrax protective antigen. In the current SCOPe 2.08 release that undivided SID is no longer present; the same chain is split into two SCOPe domains: d1acca2, residues A:14-225, in b.179.1.1, the all-beta PA14 family within the PA14-like superfamily and Anthrax protective antigen N-terminal-like fold; and d1acca1, residues A:226-735, in f.60.1.1, the Anthrax protective antigen C-terminal-like family/superfamily/fold. The revision means that chain A is a two-domain fusion: its PA14-like N-terminal region and its C-terminal protective-antigen region are separate evolutionary modules, not one homologous unit spanning the whole chain. Homology searches, template choice, and functional annotation transfer should therefore be restricted to the matching residue interval. A hit to A:14-225 supports only the PA14-like b.179.1/family b.179.1.1 region, and a hit to A:226-735 supports only the f.60.1/family f.60.1.1 region; the old full-chain SCOP 1.75 domain should not be used to infer that the two regions share a fold, family, superfamily, or directly transferable functions or boundaries with every full-length match.\",\"evidence_used\":[\"mcp__scope.get_scop_domains: /api/v1/releases/1.75/sids/d1acca_; /api/v1/releases/1.75/sids/d1acca_/parents\",\"mcp__scope.get_scop_domains: /api/v1/releases/current; /api/v1/releases/2.08/sids/d1acca_; /api/v1/releases/2.08/sids/d1acca1; /api/v1/releases/2.08/sids/d1acca1/parents; /api/v1/releases/2.08/sids/d1acca2; /api/v1/releases/2.08/sids/d1acca2/parents\"]}]}"}}
